  \n\nI think discard B2 is reasonable to simplify hand toward W and honors.  \n\nBut also, discarding J1 might be better to pursue One Voided Suit and avoid honors, but then I lose Dragon Pung chance.  \n\nGiven I have J1 pair, keeping it offers chance for Dragon Pung (2 points) and if I get another J1, that's a pung. That's easier than forming a chow with B2 or B8.  \n\nSo I should keep J1 pair.  \n\nBetween W1, W4, W8 pairs, W4 is already a pung (three tiles), so I should keep all three W4 as a concealed pung. W1 and W8 are pairs.  \n\nThus, discard B2 or B8. I'll choose B2 because B2 is lower and might be more dangerous? Actually, B2 is terminal-ish (low number), but not terminal.  \n\nLet's see history: next player played B2 earlier, so maybe safe.  \n\nI'll discard B2.  \n\nReason: Discarding B2 simplifies the hand by removing an isolated tile from a suit where I already have a meld, retaining potential pungs with W1, W4, W8, and J1 pairs. It maintains flexibility to pursue patterns like All Pungs (if I can get four pungs) or mixed hand, and avoids breaking existing valuable sets.  \n\nAnswer: Play B2"}
